Wire algebra type conflicts into errors

build_structures already collects per-rule TypeConflicts during the
populate/close loop, but build.rs threw the result away. Capture the
errors and chain them into the same merge that picks between
binding/signature/casing/eqlog errors so the user actually sees them.

To match the existing eqlog-side conflict format the algebra path now
emits a single CompileError variant: ConflictingTermType. Drop the
recently-introduced ConflictingParentEl variant and lower every
TypeConflict — both same-TypeId-with-differing-parents and outright
TypeId mismatch — through one path. ConcreteTypes render as `TypeName`
for global types or `parent_name.TypeName` for member types, with
`parent_name` looked up in the structure's var_els (or `?` for ambient
els not bound to a variable).

Two adjustments line the algebra-side output up with the existing
eqlog-side diagnostics:

  - RuleStructures gains a per-structure `native_terms` set tracking
    which term ids were inserted directly into a structure (vs.
    inherited from a clone of an earlier structure). conflict_to_error
    anchors only at native terms; without this filter the diagnostic
    points at terms cloned forward from an earlier statement, at a
    location unrelated to the conflict.

  - Structure::close's typing pass now imposes argument types across
    every func/pred app before it imposes any codomain type. emit_app
    no longer eagerly writes the codomain into els at allocation time
    — it leaves that to typing — so the existing/new ordering on a
    conflicting el matches the order the eqlog-side typing rules use,
    and the `Could be ...` lines come out in the same sequence.
